    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Named after the Y‑shaped DeSmet Junction where US 93 and MT 200 met former US 10, Wye’s name reflects its historic crossroads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wye%2C_Montana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Wye once hosted a railroad station, a key feature of its 1895 community and a reminder of the town’s transportation past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://roadsidethoughts.com/mt/wye-xx-missoula-profile.htm",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Nestled in the Bitterroot Valley, Wye is surrounded by the Bitterroot Range, which rises between 5,000 and 7,000 feet above the floor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoveringmontana.com/towns/wye/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "From the valley floor, the Continental Divide and main Rockies ridges rise to the east and northeast, framing Wye’s dramatic backdrop.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoveringmontana.com/towns/wye/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wye lies within the Montana Rockies, marking it as part of the northern extremes of the Bitterroot Valley’s rugged terrain.",
